<p>
<b><font size=4>MONITORING ACTIVITIES</font></b>
<p>
<b>Steve Bauer</b><br>
<i>Pocket Water, Inc. (PWI)</i>
<p>
During the first part of the month PWI assembled turbidity data loggers, temperature loggers, and camera gear for installation and use on-site.  PWI acquired a Hach turbidity meter and laptop computer for use on the Red River project.  We also purchased a water level meter for the project that will be used to record accurate groundwater levels over the life of the project.
<p>
PWI met with UI regarding data interpretation and GIS storage of channel response and fisheries micro-habitat data.
<p>
UI also obtained the lab apparatus and chemicals to conduct a bench test of sediment settling rates using alum as a binding agent.
<p>
PWI and Rapid Creek Research calibrated the turbidity meters using formazin standards before installation in the field.  Turbidity data loggers were installed in the turbidity test channels one day prior to the test runs.  The water pump, weirs, and turbidity data loggers all worked without a problem.
<p>
Sediment samples and flows were collected so that sediment reduction rates could be calculated.  The data evaluation is being completed by UI with assistance from PWI.  Data interpretation and conclusions should be completed in July.
<p>
The turbidity data logger and temporary gage were installed in the river following use in the turbidity test channel.  Steve and Kurt installed the equipment on June 24th prior to the large storms that came through on the following day.  Red River rose approximately a foot, rising to bankfull height at Giant Bend.  Steve estimated the flow to be nearly 450 cfs, which is quite high for this time of year.  
<p>
<center>
<font size=2>Steve (right), Kurt (left), and Gloria (middle) consult on the proper placement of a groundwater monitoring well.  The mini track excavator sits poised and ready for instructions.</font>
<img src="excavator.jpg" width=400 height=300 alt="" border="1"></center>
<p>
Water and air temperature data loggers were also installed.  This year we improved the air temperature monitoring by installing solar radiation shields.  Air temperature measurements are useful for understanding yearly trends and variations in water temperature.  These devices simply consist of a stack of plastic plates that provide the appropriate shielding from direct solar radiation, resulting in accurate air temperature measurements.
<p>
UI assisted PWI and WHI in locating the most appropriate position for groundwater monitoring wells.  Using the same auger attachment on the mini excavator employed for planting willows, UI, PWI, and WHI successfully installed the wells this year.  Kurt was able to effectively dig holes from 7 to 8 feet deep in most locations.  A total of 15 groundwater wells have been installed including a transect in the Phase III project area.  The water level meter will be used to manually collect data throughout the field season. 
<p>
